+
+# Design analysis for the D12 nested-wall variant
+# a) Concrete behavior:
+#    - Input now contains 2-3 wall components of the same color. They are not siblings spread around
+#      the canvas; they form a strict containment chain: the largest half-enclosure contains a smaller
+#      half-enclosure, which may itself contain an even smaller one.
+#    - Only the innermost cavity contains the fill-color seed. After that wall is solved, the resulting
+#      fill reaches the next cavity, making that newly reached area the seed for the next larger wall.
+#      This repeats from inner to outer until the outermost wall radiates to the grid edge.
+#    - Small example trace:
+#         original single-wall logic: seed -> fill one cavity -> radiate to border.
+#         new nested logic: seed inside wall C -> solve C, fill reaches wall B's cavity ->
+#         solve B, fill reaches wall A's cavity -> solve A, fill exits the whole nest.
+#      The difference is that choosing only the wall nearest to the original seed is no longer enough.
+#
+# b) Complexity calibration:
+#    - The variant always has at least 2 nested levels, optionally 3. This keeps the hierarchy obvious
+#      without exploding the number of cases.
+#    - All nested walls share the same fill color and wall color, so the new complexity is purely the
+#      spatial hierarchy and processing order, not extra color bookkeeping.
+#
+# c) Original-solver assumptions that break here:
+#    - `wall_comp = min(comps, ...)` assumed exactly one relevant wall. In the variant this would stop
+#      after the innermost wall and miss all outer levels.
+#    - `barrier = comp_mask.copy()` was safe only because there were no other same-color walls. Here the
+#      other wall components must remain non-passable background obstacles while the current component is
+#      being processed.
+#    - The fill seed was previously enough to identify the whole task. Now the current fill region after
+#      each stage becomes the seed for the next stage, so the solver must update and reuse it.
+#
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    - Generator keeps every child wall fully inside the parent interior and enforces one empty-cell moat
+#      in 8-neighborhood terms, so nested walls do not merge into a single component.
+#    - The deepest seed is placed only in the deepest wall interior. Obstacles are still placed on bg
+#      cells away from walls so they do not get mistaken for the fill color.
+#
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    - The hierarchy is visible directly: a small U-like wall appears inside a larger one.
+#    - The output shows stagewise expansion: inner cavity fills first, then the parent cavity, then the
+#      outer radiation. This is visible even when all levels share the same opening direction.
+#
+# f) Visual cleanliness:
+#    - The generator uses a strict nested chain rather than siblings, keeps 8-separation between levels,
+#      and uses only a few obstacle pixels, so the wall hierarchy stays readable.
+#
+# g) Color inference:
+#    - No ordering depends on color ids. Fill color is still inferred structurally as the non-wall color
+#      closest to the wall system, because the seed is the only non-wall color touching the deepest wall.
+#
+# Main new assumptions and how generation guarantees them:
+#    - Assumption: wall components form a reachable inner-to-outer chain. Guarantee: generation builds a
+#      recursive containment chain only, and rejects samples where any stage adds zero fill.
+#    - Assumption: each wall component still has exactly two degree-1 endpoints. Guarantee: wall builder
+#      preserves the original endpoint constraint at every nesting level.
+#    - Assumption: the nearest unprocessed wall to the current fill region is the next nesting level.
+#      Guarantee: no sibling walls are generated, only a single containment chain.
